WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:05.860
Hi, everyone. I'm Clive Loseby

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:08.740
and I am passionate
about changing the world,

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:10.380
one website at a time.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:13.380
As Dale said I was
in a bad car crash a long time ago.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:16.460
It left us all
with life-changing injuries.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:20.020
And that's why accessibility
and website accessibility

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:22.020
has become my passion.

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:24.620
The accident also left me
with a very bad memory,

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:27.260
so please forgive me
if I keep looking at my notes.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:32.620
OK. Does everybody know
what a cookie banner is?

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.500
Those annoying banners
that pop up on a website

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:40.940
asking for consent
to put cookies into your browser?

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.500
It may say something like,
"This website uses cookies

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.860
to give you the best online experience.

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:48.660
Follow this link to confirm
that you consent to this.”

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:50.700
Does everyone know what I'm talking about?

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:51.700
(Audience: Yes!)

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:53.820
Good. Right, now I want you to imagine

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:55.300
a different kind of browser.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.500
This one says the following:

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:00.500
"This website is designed
for white people only.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:04.020
Follow this link
to confirm that you are white."

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:09.940
Or, "This website is for men only.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:12.700
Follow this link
to confirm that you are a man."

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:17.620
Or, "This website
is for straight people only.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:20.540
Follow this link to confirm
that you are straight."

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:24.380
Or even, "This website
is for people who have hair.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:28.460
Follow this link
to confirm you have hair."

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:29.780
OK.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:31.620
These don't happen in the real world,

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:35.540
but what I do need to tell you about
is another banner that does exist

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.660
on over 97% of the world's websites.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:43.260
"This website is for people
who are not disabled.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:46.940
Follow this link to confirm
that you are not disabled."

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.740
This may not be as apparent
as the cookie banner.

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:51.660
Believe me, this banner exists.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:57.500
Ninety-seven point four percent 
of 1 million website home pages

00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:59.180
tested last year

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:02.620
had detectable accessibility failures.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:07.420
So how many disabled people
are there in the world?

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.460
In 2010, the World Health Organization

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:12.300
estimated that 1 billion 
people on the planet

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:14.620
live with some form of disability,

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:20.500
with over 110 million having highly
significant difficulties in functioning.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:23.580
We are talking about a lot of people.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:29.460
They also would like to use
the web as much as you or I do.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:33.540
They also want to become your customers
and spend money with you,

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.260
if you make it possible for them to do so.

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:43.700
The title of this talk is a direct quote
from somebody I met last year.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:47.180
She works in health, safety,
employee well-being

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:49.340
and has done for 20 years,

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:51.660
so she has a real understanding
of inclusivity.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:53.820
Lovely, caring person,

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:58.020
but she just could not understand
how blind people could use the internet

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:00.260
because they couldn't see the screen.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:03.300
I explained to her
what a screen reader was

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:04.620
and she got it.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:06.220
A screen reader, by the way,

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:10.500
is a piece of software
that reads out the content of a website

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:12.220
using a computerized voice.

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:17.220
If you're blind, you can't use a mouse
because you can't see where to put it.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:22.180
So you will use your keyboard instead
in conjunction with the screen reader.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:24.020
It's not that easy to do

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:28.220
but cookie banners
make life so much worse.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:31.380
And in some cases,
they actually prevent blind people

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:34.260
from accessing the website beyond it.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:38.020
We are in the year 2022.

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:40.140
The first set of guidelines for websites

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:43.820
to help ensure that they met
the needs of disabled people

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:48.820
were released quite recently,
actually, in 1999.

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:52.220
A mere 23 years ago.

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:54.140
Yep. I'm not joking.

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:58.340
All the information has been available
to everyone since then.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:01.820
It is also a legal requirement
in most countries,

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:03.220
but above everything else,

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:06.060
it helps every business
and every organization

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:07.980
become more inclusive.

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:11.900
And yet, 97% of websites

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:15.900
contained detectable
accessibility failures last year.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:17.900
Think of a website as a building,

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:19.340
it becomes even more obvious.

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:21.420
After all, websites are
physical buildings.

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:24.260
They're your window
on the world, aren't they?

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:27.060
Legislation for access
to physical buildings

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:30.540
has always made allowances
for very old buildings

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:34.220
where it's simply not possible
to make them fully accessible

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:35.980
for people with disabilities.

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:37.660
We all understand that.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:39.820
So actually, I think it's perfectly fair

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:43.900
that you shouldn't expect
a website over 23 years old

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:46.300
to cater for disabled people either.

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:49.380
I'm actually not sure
the last time I went on a website

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:51.380
that was over 23 years old.

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:54.020
But what about five years old
or about three years old?

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:55.620
What about last year? This year?

00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:59.620
Typically, websites get replaced
every three to five years

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:03.220
as a new one is built
to cater for the latest trends.

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:07.180
Is it really OK that websites
that are recently built

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:10.460
are still inaccessible
for disabled people?

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:12.660
If not now, when?

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:18.100
OK, so what does accessible
web design actually mean?

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:21.260
Please take a look at these doors.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:23.700
They both go into the same building.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:26.620
Both sets of doors have level access.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:30.420
The door on the left slides open
automatically as you approach it.

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:33.420
The doors on the right
have to be pulled open.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:36.540
I would like to ask you all a question,

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:37.860
and if you are able,

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:40.540
I would like you
to either raise your left hand

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:43.420
or your right hand in answer.

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:47.460
Imagine you're in a wheelchair
and you want to enter this building.

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:52.460
Which of the doors do you think
you would be most likely to use?

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:55.980
The automatic doors on the left
or the heavy doors on the right?

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:59.060
Could you please raise your left
or your right hand accordingly?

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:05.140
Well, I think it's fair to say
the consensus is on the left. Interesting.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:10.380
OK. Now instead of being in a wheelchair,
you're using a walking frame,

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:11.700
so you can walk,

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:14.260
but you need to have both
of your hands on the frame.

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:18.060
Which set of doors do you think
you would be likely to use now?

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:20.820
Could you raise your hands
if you're able to?

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:22.940
I'm really surprised.
Everyone's going left.

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:25.380
OK. Right.

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:29.460
Now you're pushing a child in a pushchair
so you can walk quite normally,

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:33.140
but you've got a pushchair
and both of your hands on it.

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:34.420
Which set of doors now?

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:36.180
Left or right?

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:38.300
I'm staggered.

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:40.740
OK. Final example.

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:44.420
Let's assume that you've actually gone in
through the doors on the right.

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:47.020
You're coming out,
you want to come out the building,

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:49.860
but you've actually got
boxes of files under both arms.

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:51.660
Which of the doors would you go then?

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:53.980
Yeah. Hmm.

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:55.300
I'm really staggered.

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:56.740
In pretty much every example

00:06:56.740 --> 00:06:58.860
pretty much everybody
raised your left hand.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:00.140
Why was this?

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:03.980
After all, you’re free to choose
which door to use at each time.

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:06.940
Why would so many people
go for an automatic door?

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:10.060
Maybe because it's easier.

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:12.020
Nobody's forcing you to use it.

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:15.180
It's there as an alternative
to the other doors.

00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:17.740
Accessible web design is exactly the same,

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:20.300
offering people with different needs

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:24.300
alternative ways of using a website
to get the same information.

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:28.060
Most countries around the world
have anti-discrimination laws,

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:29.820
and websites fall under these laws.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:33.300
However, for me, it's not
about complying with the law.

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:36.900
It is about becoming more inclusive,
more welcoming to everyone,

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:40.300
regardless of their ability
or their disability.

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:43.980
Disabled people have money to spend
on your goods and services

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:46.020
and if they can't spend it with you,

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:48.220
they're going to spend it
with somebody else.

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:53.140
So what should you do
with regard to your own website?

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:56.980
Well, you need to have two things.

00:07:57.020 --> 00:07:59.060
You need to have an audit of your website,

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:00.380
and then you need to put

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:02.500
an accessibility statement
on your website.

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:04.660
I'll explain what I mean
in this photograph.

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:06.980
This hotel, it's a beautiful building.

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:09.540
There’s downstairs seating.
There’s upstairs seating.

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:14.140
There's beautiful staircases everywhere,
but unfortunately, there isn't a lift.

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:17.860
The restaurant itself is run
by an outside catering company.

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:20.260
It's not their fault there's no lift.

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:22.940
It's not their building,
they're just renting the space.

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:26.660
However, if you're in a wheelchair,
you can't use the upstairs seating.

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:31.940
But if there was a sign near the entrance
saying something like,

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:36.340
"Patrons in wheelchairs, please note
that although we've got upstairs seating,

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:37.900
we do not have a lift.

00:08:37.900 --> 00:08:41.500
However, we do have
some reserved seating for you

00:08:41.540 --> 00:08:45.940
with extra turning space
and raised tables."

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:47.260
Think about raised tables.

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:51.020
If you're in a wheelchair and you try
to go into a restaurant anywhere,

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:53.340
you can't quite get under the table.

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:56.340
Easily fixed, just put them on
some small wooden blocks.

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:58.380
Anyway, if you just put up that sign,

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:00.620
somebody in a wheelchair
who reads that sign,

00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:02.820
are they going to feel
more or less included?

00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:08.100
The cost of doing this is almost nothing,
but the effect is huge.

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:10.780
It's not the catering companies
fault there's no lift.

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:14.180
They've just gone out of their way
to offer an alternative

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:16.540
despite the limitations of the hotel.

00:09:16.580 --> 00:09:19.420
In other words,
the catering company themselves

00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:22.740
have become more welcoming,
inclusive and accessible.

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:25.300
So the first part of this story

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:28.460
where I explained the restaurant
has upstairs seating, but no lift.

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:30.300
That could be seen as an audit.

00:09:30.340 --> 00:09:32.740
"We realize there's no lift
to the upstairs seating

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:35.620
so disabled people
in wheelchairs cannot use it."

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:38.660
But the sign at the entrance
telling everyone

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:42.620
that there is accessible
seating downstairs as an alternative

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:44.860
is like their accessibility statement.

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:48.260
"We've discovered that wheelchair users
cannot use the upstairs seating.

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:50.220
This is what we've done instead."

00:09:50.260 --> 00:09:54.140
So how does all this apply to a website?

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:55.820
Well, as far as the audit goes,

00:09:55.820 --> 00:09:58.380
you can either pay somebody
or you can do it yourself.

00:09:58.420 --> 00:10:00.860
And the same goes
for an accessibility statement.

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:03.100
Sadly, I don’t have time
to go into the details

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:05.020
but I do want to say one thing.

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:08.180
Make your accessibility statement real.

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:11.340
What do I mean by this? Simple.

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:14.540
I see website after website
with an accessibility statement

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:18.500
that begins with a slightly pompous tone,
and it says something like,

00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:20.980
"We fully care about
people with disabilities.

00:10:21.020 --> 00:10:24.620
We want to ensure that everyone
is able to fully utilize our website.

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:28.900
It meets the following standards,
blah, blah, blah."

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:31.700
I don't actually really care what you say.

00:10:31.700 --> 00:10:35.140
If somebody can't use your website,
they can't use your website.

00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:36.700
I've got an idea.

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:40.020
Why not instead use this
as a wonderful opportunity

00:10:40.060 --> 00:10:43.020
to reach out to disabled people?

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:45.220
The first line of your
accessibility statement

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:46.500
could be something like,

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:49.260
“We’re aware that there’s
some problems with our website

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:50.820
that might make the experience

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:53.740
more difficult for visitors
with disabilities.

00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:57.220
But if there’s anything that we can do
to help you, give us a call.”

00:10:57.220 --> 00:10:59.060
There's the phone number right there.

00:10:59.060 --> 00:11:02.180
“Follow this link to send an email.”
There’s the link right there.

00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:03.620
“Or fill in the contact form.”

00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:05.540
Right there you're reaching out to them.

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:07.660
Just by doing that,
you're being inclusive.

00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:09.500
You're going the extra mile

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:13.020
and it speaks volumes
about your ethos as a company.

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:17.260
By the way, if you do have
accessibility issues with your website,

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:21.060
you should give a time frame
in which you're going to get them fixed

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:24.460
or at least explain that
it’s not possible on grounds of cost.

00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:29.380
It isn't about being perfect.
It's about being honest.

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:33.620
OK, I'd like to finish by telling you
all about a very simple test

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:36.140
that you can do on any website

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:39.460
to find out if it has
a basic level of accessibility.

00:11:39.500 --> 00:11:40.860
Anyone can do it.

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:42.860
And I call it the Tab Test.

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:45.420
If you're on a website,
there will usually be

00:11:45.460 --> 00:11:48.620
a row of page navigation
buttons along the top.

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:51.220
As you move your mouse over them,

00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:53.700
they will light up in turn

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:56.620
and any subpages will appear
as a kind of drop-down.

00:11:56.620 --> 00:11:59.740
And they may also light up
as you move the mouse over.

00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:02.740
In other words, just by moving your mouse,

00:12:02.740 --> 00:12:06.300
you can go to pretty much any page
on the website with a single click.

00:12:06.340 --> 00:12:08.500
Do we all understand that?

00:12:08.540 --> 00:12:10.060
Brilliant.

00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:12.740
If you're blind, as I've said,
you can't use a mouse.

00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:15.780
Instead, you use a screen reader
and a keyboard.

00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:19.220
If a website has
a basic level of accessibility,

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:24.020
you should be able to select
every page and subpage

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:26.820
by repeatedly pressing the Tab key.

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:29.940
Not only that, each one
should light up in turn,

00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:32.100
just as if you were using a mouse.

00:12:33.020 --> 00:12:36.980
Not everyone who is visually impaired
is completely blind.

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:40.460
In fact, most of them have
some form of limited vision instead.

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:45.020
So having page navigation
light up as you move,

00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:46.460
as you Tab through them,

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:48.780
helps them know where they are.

00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:54.180
So next time anybody here is on a website,
just start pressing the Tab key.

00:12:54.220 --> 00:12:59.300
If you do not see all the pages
and all the subpages light up in turn,

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:00.980
it's failed the test.

00:13:01.020 --> 00:13:04.940
Please drop them an email
informing them that because their website

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:08.020
does not meet that basic
level of accessibility,

00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:11.660
it's failed this test and they really
need to do something about it.

00:13:11.660 --> 00:13:14.460
They want to know more,
they can do a quick internet search

00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:17.220
and actually it's fairly easy to fix.

00:13:18.620 --> 00:13:22.100
If you guys joined with me
in doing this one simple thing,

00:13:23.180 --> 00:13:27.660
we all will truly be changing the world,
one website at a time.

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:28.940
Thank you very much.

00:13:28.940 --> 00:13:30.300
(Applause)

